1. OFFICIAL NOTICES FOR USERS

1.1 Motivation for this release

Since the last release (2.2.1) of FreeEOS in 2008 (!)  many
stellar-interior groups have chosen to adopt FreeEOS to provide the
EOS data they need to calculate their models since if you adopt exact
OPAL abundances, it replicates OPAL results well (especially the OPAL
solar results) through much of the physical conditions covered by
those results.  Furthermore, FreeEOS can be used to extrapolate
smoothly from OPAL results for different abundances and a wider range
of physical conditions than used in the OPAL calculations.  Note, however,
all the normal extrapolation caveats.

Therefore, because of this on-going interest in FreeEOS, the primary
goal for this current release (and the development effort that lead up
to it) is to substantially improve the reliability of FreeEOS
*without* changing the free-energy semiempirical models that are the
basis of the various EOS option suites that are available with
FreeEOS.  So 3.0.0 should provide much more reliable convergence than
2.2.1, but users should expect essentially the same results for 3.0.0
and 2.2.1 if their prior use of 2.2.1 happened by chance to converge.

To help support this improved reliability goal, I have completely
converted the Fortran 77 (with extensions) code present in 2.2.1 to
Fortran 2008 (which is much easier Fortran standard for me to use to
debug and develop FreeEOS); implemented a comprehensive test system
(which tests FreeEOS supported option suites over a large range of
physical conditions); and greatly extended the CMake-based build
system to support these improvements.  See "Development Topics" below
for details of these and other changes.

1.2 Building applications and libraries that depend on FreeEOS

Because the Fortran 77 code used for 2.2.1 has been upgraded to
Fortran 2008 for 3.0.0 and because the CMake-based build system for
2.2.1 has been substantially upgraded for 3.0.0, the simple steps
required to build an application or library against an installed
version of FreeEOS-3.0.0 have been substantially changed.  Therefore,
consult the INSTALL file for how to build and install FreeEOS-3.0.0
and link your applications to the installed library.

1.3 Fortran compilers that are compatible with FreeEOS

FreeEOS is written to the Fortran 2008 standard so we feel confident
that any Fortran compiler that supports that standard should work well
with FreeEOS.  That said, FreeEOS has been primarily developed using
the gfortran compiler, and for this release we have also made sure
that all test targets pass for the ifort and nagfor compilers.  Note
that nagfor is particularly well known for good error checking so the
good results for that compiler alone as well as the good results for
gfortran and ifort add to the above confidence.

1.4 CMake minimum version bumped to 3.18.4

    This value represents a compromise between which CMake version (or
    greater) is currently supplied in binary form by the latest
    versions of most free software distributions and the desire to
    maximize the CMake version to reduce CMake bugs and provide
    additional CMake functionality.  Note, if your free software
    distribution does not provide this version (or greater) of CMake
    in binary form, it is straightforward to build the latest version
    of CMake following the directions at <https://cmake.org/install/>.

2. DEVELOPMENT TOPICS FOR THIS RELEASE

2.1 Converted from svn to git revision control

  This conversion was implemented in a way that transformed all
  commits in the svn repository to the equivalent git commits.  That
  svn repository itself was created originally as a conversion of a
  CVS repository that preserved all the CVS commits in that repository
  as the equivalent svn commits.  As a result the git repository for
  FreeEOS contains commits that track development of that software
  from 2004-09-09 onwards.

2.2 Fixed *many* bugs

  FreeEOS-3.0.0 has been written strictly to the Fortran
  2008 standard and using best practices for that standard (see
  below).  This allows detection and fixing of many bugs that were
  present in FreeEOS-2.2.1 (which was written to an extended version
  of the Fortran 77 standard).

  For example, the old version of the code used generic saves for
  every variable of almost all procedures which covered up a whole
  bunch of out-of-order calculations in each iteration of the EOS
  which have now been fixed.  The new code only uses saves where they
  are strictly necessary (e.g., saving state information that helps
  start new EOS calculations for physical conditions that are not that
  different from those from the previous call).  The result of this
  kind of bug fix is the initial EOS iteration is much more reliable
  now (since no uninitialized variables are used for that iteration),
  and the rate of convergence should be improved as well since the
  iteration calculation is no longer relying on out-of-date saved
  variables from the previous iteration to work around out-of-order
  calculation issues.

  As part of this large bug-fixing effort we used NaN tainting of reals
  and huge tainting of integers to detect (and fix) many uninitialized
  variable issues.

  For more complete details of all bugs fixed see the ChangeLog.release file.

2.3 Converted Fortran source code from fixed to free format

  The external change here is the Fortran source code filenames have
  been changed from *.f to *.f90 form to help inform compilers that
  the Fortran source is in free format.  The most obvious internal
  changes are the continuation style (ampersand ending the line
  preceding the continuation line) and taking advantage of the longer
  line length possible with free format to reduce the number of
  continuations.

2.4 Updated the FreeEOS code standard from (extended) Fortran 77 to Fortran 2008

  The compliance with the Fortran 2008 standard has been confirmed by no
  warnings due to the gfortran -std=f2008, ifort -std08, and nagfor
  -f2008 options.

2.5 Implemented Fortran 2008 best practices

  See README.best_practices for the details of these changes.
  N.B. In the interest of making this release in a timely manner, the
  current code is not 100 per cent compliant with those stated best
  practices.  But it is pretty close to that goal.

2.6 Implemented configuration of the floating-point representation

  This change has been implemented for FreeEOS via the CMake variables
  supported_fp_precision and supported_fp_exponent where the default
  values of these variables (15 and 300) correspond to the usual IEEE
  754 "binary64" precision (often known simply as "double precision")
  which is likely what virtually every user prefers to use since for
  most computer hardware this gives the fastest results with
  reasonable numerical precision and dynamic range.

  However, for special development purposes (e.g., checking the code
  for derivative errors by comparing results with derivatives
  determined by numerical differences as in free_eos_test (see
  test_FreeEOS/README.free_eos_test for further discussion of that
  application) or checking the code for any underflows that change
  results in any significant way) it is useful to specify non-default
  supported_fp_precision and supported_fp_exponent values of 18 and
  4900 corresponding to the IEEE 754 "x87" extended precision
  representation (often known simply as "extended precision") or 32
  and 4900 corresponding to IEEE 754 "binary128" precision (often
  known simply as "quadruple precision".  N.B. to take full advantage
  of the improved precision and dynamic range of these additional
  floating-point representations, it is necessary to build special
  versions of lapack and blas with these same floating-point
  representations as well, and link the free_eos library to whatever
  special version of lapack and blas is appropriate.

2.7 Updated the physical constants from CODATA 2002 to CODATA 2018

  As previously, the physical constants used by FreeEOS are divided into 7
  primary constants and the remaining secondary constants which are
  calculated from the primary constants with the maximum precision
  possible (typically 16 figures of precision for double precision)
  with the adopted floating-point representation. This insures the
  rounding that occurs in the CODATA 2018 values of the secondary
  constants and the (small) losses in the thermodynamic consistency of
  the FreeEOS results that would occur due to that rounding does not
  cover up any other source of thermodynamic inconsistency in FreeEOS
  results.

  Five of the 7 primary constants are exact with only the Rydberg and
  the proton mass still being experimentally determined.  However,
  these two constants are currently specified in CODATA 2018 to
  respectively 14 and 13 significant digits so I anticipate future
  changes in the physical constants adopted for FreeEOS will be quite
  small except for the future molar mass constant change discussed below.

  Since the free-energy model has not changed between FreeEOS-2.2.1
  and this release (3.0.0), and I have been careful to check for most
  of the extensive bug fixes that no significant differences were
  introduced in *.stdout free_eos_test results from building the
  simple_test, valgrind_test, and comprehensive_test targets and *.png
  figure results from building the FreeEOS_papers target, I ascribe
  most differences in results between FreeEOS-2.2.1 and this release to
  these changes in physical constants and ionization potentials.  It
  turns out that for kif=2 and EOS1 (the most reliable variant of
  FreeEOS-2.2.1), the maximum such relative change in P(rho, T) over
  the log rho-log T plane is only of order 10^{-4.5}, see
  <http://freeeos.sourceforge.net/3.0.0-2.2.1_EOS1.png>.  Those
  changes are small relative to actual FreeEOS errors, and I assume
  future changes in physical constants will have an even smaller effect.

  N.B. In the interest of releasing FreeEOS-3.0.0 in a timely manner,
  the physical constants in that software are derived and used
  implicitly assuming the SI molar mass constant is 1.×10−3 kg⋅mol−1
  while according to
  <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Molar_mass_constant> the 2018 CODATA
  recommended value of this measured constant is actually
  0.99999999965(30)×10−3 kg⋅mol−1.  After the 3.0.0 release I plan to
  change the FreeEOS code so the molar mass constant is explicitly
  used in a consistent manner, and I further plan to change from its
  current implicit value to the correct 2018 CODATA value.  But
  meanwhile the relative errors caused by this (implicit)
  approximation should be of order 10^{-10}, i.e., negligible.

2.8 Updated the ionization potentials to the latest NIST values

  In this case "latest" refers to Kramida, A., Ralchenko, Yu., Reader,
  J., and NIST ASD Team (2019). NIST Atomic Spectra Database
  (ver. 5.7.1), [Online]. Available: https://physics.nist.gov/asd
  [2020, March 11]. National Institute of Standards and Technology,
  Gaithersburg, MD. DOI: https://doi.org/10.18434/T4W30F

  The above figure does not show any obvious local residuals due to
  ionization zones so it appears this change has introduced smaller
  changes than those due to the above change in physical constants.

2.9 Updated isotopic masses to AME 2020

I got these data from electronic files made available by the Argonne
National Laboratory site,
<https://www.anl.gov/phy/reference/ame-2020-massmas20>.  That site
recommends the following reference for this work:

"The AME 2020 atomic mass evaluation has been published in Chinese
 Phys. C 45, 030002 (2021) and Chinese Phys. C 45, 030003 (2021). Any
 work that will use these files should make reference to the original
 papers listed above, and not to the electronic files."

2.10 Implemented a verbosity argument (just for the modern free_eos API)

  This free_eos argument implements control of how much output to
  stderr is allowed by the free_eos library.

  + verbosity.eq.0 corresponds to no stderr output allowed for the
    free_eos library other than some extra stderr output that sometimes
    occurs just before error stop statements that are themselves
    the result of a failed sanity check (i.e., an unexpected internal
    logic inconsistency or an unexpected combination of argument values
    for the free_eos call.

  + verbosity.ge.1 corresponds to allowing minimal stderr output from the
    free_eos library that is associated with errors, i.e., non-zero info
    values.

  + verbosity.ge.2 corresponds to allowing stderr output from the
    free_eos library that is associated with warnings, i.e.,
    problematic issues that are not sufficiently severe to cause a
    non-zero info value.

  + verbosity.ge.3 corresponds to allowing stderr output from the
    free_eos library that is associated with more important
    informational messages (e.g., debug output when the BFGS minimization
    technique is used).

  + verbosity.ge.4 was originally implemented to allow stderr output
    from the free_eos library that is associated with the least
    important informational (debug) messages that corresponded to all
    previously disabled (i.e, commented out or specified with Boolean
    logic that is never true) stderr output.  However, the results
    required a punishing amount of disk space, and were often
    crude/not useful (i.e., those output statements were disabled for
    a reason).  Therefore, the code was further changed so that
    verbosity.ge.4 had the same effect as verbosity.ge.3, i.e., all
    verbosity.ge.4 logic was replaced by .false..and.verbosity.ge.4 to
    mark output code that *might* be useful for debugging purposes in
    the future but which is not used now.

2.11 Implemented the qe and qv arguments (just for the modern free_eos API)

  The returned values of these free_eos arguments allow routines that
  call that subroutine to calculate in a convenient manner the
  gravitational energy generation rate according to the fundamental
  principles discussed in Strittmatter, P.A., Faulkner, J.. Robertson,
  J.W., and Faulkner, D.J. 1970 Ap.J. 161, 369-373.

2.12 Implemented the info argument (just for the modern free_eos API)

  All routines that call the modern API form of free_eos should check
  the returned value of info.  If that value is non-zero, that indicates
  an abnormal termination of the free_eos call with most returned values
  invalid or undefined.

2.13 Implemented the sound2 (square of the sound speed) argument (just for the modern free_eos API)

2.13 Changed how the "stop" and "error stop" statements are used
  internally by the free_eos library

  Internal logic errors are now terminated with the "error stop"
  statement rather than the "stop" statement.  Such logic errors are
  typically detected by sanity checks.  Note, this is now the only use
  of the "error stop" statement within the free_eos library, and there
  is now no use of the stop statement within that library (e.g., EOS error
  conditions that have nothing to do with sanity checks no longer execute
  "stop".  Instead, such error conditions are signalled with
  non-zero info values.

  As a result of these changes, applications that link to the free_eos
  library (e.g., free_eos_test) can use the stop statement for normal
  terminations (which by default for gfortran yields an extremely
  useful floating-point exception report), and take any other measure
  they like (e.g., executing the "error stop" statement or starting a
  new series of EOS calculations for a different isochore, isobar, or
  isotherm) when non-zero info values are returned.

2.14 Implemented source code documentation

  This project has been implemented with doxygen.  The current status
  is all modules and module procedures have doxygen summary
  documentation, but only a small subset of the procedures (which does
  include the two API's for the important free_eos generic subroutine)
  currently have doxygen argument documentation, and there is no
  doxygen documentation of module variables.  So I have made a good
  start with this project with results that can be browsed (e.g., for
  the unpacked 3.0.0 tarball) at either
  free_eos-3.0.0/document_FreeEOS/doxygen/doxygen/latex/FreeEOS_developer_documentation.pdf
  or free_eos-3.0.0/document_FreeEOS/doxygen/doxygen/html/index.xhtml.
  However, although that documentation is already quite extensive
  there is still obviously a large effort required to complete this
  project.

2.15 Implemented a new modern CMake-based build system

  CMake is used to configure the following tasks:

  + build the free_eos library using the "free_eos" target;

  + build the doxygen documentation (see above) using the
    "build_doxygen" target;

  + build (using modern pdflatex/aastex methods) the 5 documentation
    papers including all their figures and the utilities required to
    help create those figures using the "FreeEOS_papers" target; and

  + build the "simple_test", "valgrind_test", and
    "comprehensive_test" test targets.

    Building the "simple_test" target runs the free_eos_test,
    exchange_test, and fermi_dirac_test utilities in specific ways
    (controlled by a configured file used for stdin) and the
    rosenbrock_test, and solve_linear_test utilities as well.  In
    these cases, all results are compared exactly with results already
    generated by gfortran 8.3.0 on my platform so this test normally
    succeeds on my platform and fails those comparisons on other
    platforms where floating-point calculations have slightly
    different results than mine because of, for example, a different
    order of computation caused by a non-gfortran compiler or even
    different version of gfortran.

    Building the "valgrind_test" target tests for free_eos library
    memory management issues by running free_eos_test under valgrind
    for 15 combinations of option suites and kif values for single
    (i.e., cold start) physical conditions which have sufficient
    pressure ionization to make convergence difficult.

    Building the "comprehensive_test" target tests for convergence and
    derivative issues by running free_eos_test under an extremely wide
    range of physical conditions for the most important combinations
    of option suites and kif values.

2.16 Reduced counts of serious floating-point exceptions to zero for all test targets

  What motivated this overflow exception cleanup was the summary of
  floating-point exceptions typically provided by gfortran by default
  for the stop statement.  Originally those summaries provided reports
  of substantial numbers of the the serious floating-point exceptions
  (overflow, divide by zero, and invalid).  But by improving the
  scaling methods, pinning values that would overflow to large values
  that don't overflow and which do not disrupt convergence, erroring
  out for some overflows, and by performing a substantial rewrite of
  eos_calc that eliminated scaling inconsistencies in that routine, I
  was able to reduce the counts of these serious floating-point
  exceptions to zero for the "simple_test", "valgrind_test",
  "comprehensive_test", and FreeEOS_papers (especially the convergence
  paper which generates figures for physical conditions that are more
  extreme than for any other test) test targets.

  N.B. In the interest of releasing FreeEOS-3.0.0 in a timely manner,
  I only made this cleanup for the default double precision case.
  That is a good start, but there is some preliminary evidence that
  there is more work to do to reduce the counts of serious
  floating-point exceptions for the above test targets to zero for the
  extended and quaduple precision cases.

2.17 Rationalized the many different non-zero info return values

     These non-zero values now signal many different error conditions
     within the free_eos library.  To help organize all this error
     condition information, I introduced a unique info offset for each
     different free_eos library routine to help identify which routine
     is reporting a non-zero info value.
